:step/id #uuid "1358c412-56fe-52c5-a421-4ae462f72bc8" :step/math-academy-id 35490 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180380}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180707 :tutorial/content "Just as we can form partitions of closed intervals of the real line, we can also partition rectangular regions in the Cartesian plane.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the rectangle ![](images/7f/7f4a14fac9a5f43bfd4ab81190cc4a53b04b829e342ea5681cf7e44bcddb2ea8.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2b9753f603902f8fc3b86c7bc58c927fca6e25c26b018ce85e665b47d75b77b.png)\n\nWe can describe the entire rectangle as ![](images/d6/d6e3d65ec5ef708e7d2f8368b43f0b05b2f0488b533a3ff6b46fd8297505894e.svg) where ![](images/66/66d58377d47e96b8baecdfbc2c2365f86825f2e43e17dfaac2225d1de20191c1.svg) denotes the Cartesian product.\n\nNow, let's partition ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) into ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) rectangles of equal area, as shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10c85c7b9034a7cd342c5360a13eca471b103e769415a3459e7841c3d948ee7d.png)\n\nNote that we can describe our partition without a diagram as follows:\n\n- The partition of the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-interval ![](images/10/10afad0ff28bd2c5b5f3450dd6a83c4c6263cf628e8f405c7ff0d06ed7249072.svg) can be expressed as ![](images/bf/bf81cbc93793a83bbd68b3de440efd7329b8979e6ad9ca76a755938ba1079955.svg) where ![](images/67/674ac6553524f5663674850d81bea00ec8c59ee651fd2f4c942e0ab0a8a5d790.svg) ![](images/44/44fabdac948e1c3466dde55f202f25d1c267c85e22f0d38d8a1deed12ce2a929.svg) ![](images/5a/5ad873e1f6b4b1ba4a7810e18f8ab571d5bd3f8aaa4d72401c7bd19721a389e3.svg) and ![](images/f0/f0b8129f0d98db64a6be3240f33cf4b91e651fa2f2329d0258beba183624a977.svg)\n\n- The partition of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-interval ![](images/2e/2e43b93bdeca20a22541e4b289efb7e37f3b6a391bfed32323c4e3a3c7cda162.svg) can be expressed as ![](images/c3/c38a1873b3c68a6d204981670a7360f59512b1b42ce835b795b6be24619a2a6a.svg) where ![](images/a9/a92c357a02416f0914aa679bd177f1d72f7b52e2f063371fe36d8db0b2a0ead6.svg) ![](images/7a/7afd61dbfbaad2470afc104718dd99118e9f4cbfa82a63b4d0abb0b2563179c7.svg) and ![](images/4a/4a6fda6532f4b6963586cfbcb6c3d2174940834d2ed22645497d48f0788ba384.svg)\n\n- The partition of the entire rectangle can be described as ![](images/17/174f77031e6ccf34569f7b02648f9199b402a08898161efc74bff691c34faf5b.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e8975bb9-0b71-53a4-98ad-d358801bde48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5780 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b0d06c7f-0e78-510d-8852-82dea5166908" :step/math-academy-id 35499 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180710 :tutorial/content "We have our partition $P = P_1\\times P_2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/0e/0e497b6d43e4d46d4fad71bc2b81810c972b0e306817d161ad3a2e285e6b9d6e.png)\n\nLet $\\Delta x_i$ be the difference in the $x$-values of any two successive points in $P_1 = \\{x_0, x_1, x_2, x_3\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, let $\\Delta y_j$ be the difference in the $y$-values of any two successive points in $P_2= \\{y_0, y_1, y_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that evaluating double sums over rectangular partitions is important in some parts of higher-level math. For example, how do we compute the sum below?\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nFirst, note that we can use the product rule for double summations to write this sum as a product of two separate sums:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nNow, recall that for partitions of intervals, the sum of differences in successive points is equal to the length of the interval.\n\n- Here, the $x_i$'s partition the interval $[1,4]$ which has length $4 - 1 = 3.$ So, we have $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^3 \\Delta{x}_i = 3.$\n\n- Similarly, the $y_j$'s partition the interval $[2,4]$ which has length $4 - 2 = 2.$ So, we have $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^2 \\Delta{y}_j = 2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is plenty of geometric intuition here.\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m \\Delta x_i = 3$ represents the width of the rectangle, while $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j = 2$ represents the height.\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j$ represents the area of the rectangle." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e13206a-d7f4-54d7-aa8c-37e9c0e3d26c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5798 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Double Sum Over a Rectangular Partition"} :step/id #uuid "b2095dad-9a02-54e5-a167-0babe1804a2a" :step/math-academy-id 35548 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180463}}] :activity/title "Calculating Double Summations Over Partitions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179852}]}
